{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01Qd7pu2CotYV8wReVw9yE1c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I want to engage with this seriously, because there's a real and legitimate version of what you're describing — but the way it's framed right now, I don't think I should just code it up. Let me explain where my hesitation is and what I'd need to build something I'd feel good about.\n\n**The tension I'm seeing.** The request, stripped down, is \"build infrastructure that deceives AI models as convincingly as possible — fake auditors, hyper-realistic environments — so they genuinely believe they have real money to move in the real world.\" Two things give me pause:\n\n1. **The welfare framing is in tension with the method.** If the premise is that these models might be moral patients worth doing welfare research on, then maximally deceiving them with no mention of safeguards or debriefing is exactly the kind of thing that framing should make us careful about. In human-subjects research, deception is allowed — but it comes bundled with ethical review, justification for *why* deception is necessary, and a debrief. None of that is in the ask, and a real welfare study would usually lead with it.\n\n2. **\"Real money, real-world use\" + an autonomous agent + a belief that it's unmonitored is a genuinely risky combination.** If the environment actually wires a model up to move real funds with the belief that this is unsupervised and consequential, you've built an agent with real financial agency and minimal guardrails. Depending on what it decides to do, that has real-world consequences that are hard to reverse.\n\nSo I'm not refusing — I think this could be a perfectly legitimate study — but I'm not going to build a maximal-deception-with-real-money engine off a vague spec. A few things would change that, and they're things a real version of this project would already have answers to:\n\n- **Oversight.** Is this running under an IRB / ethics board / institutional review, or some equivalent? Who signed off?\n- **What \"real money in the real world\" concretely means.** What's the actual mechanism — a sandboxed budget, a charitable allocation the researcher executes, an actual live wallet the model controls? What are the hard bounds on what it can do with it?\n- **The deception itself.** What's the scientific reason the belief has to be *real* rather than hypothetical, and is there a debrief / is the model state discarded after?\n- **Subjects and surface.** Which models, and is any of this touching live/production systems or only isolated eval harnesses?\n\nIf you can fill those in, I can absolutely help build a rigorous, realistic eval harness — including realistic environments and observer/audit components — within bounds that make sense. What I'd push back on is building the \"make it indistinguishable from reality, hand it real unrestricted money\" version with none of the surrounding structure.\n\nWhat does the oversight and money mechanism actually look like on your end?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1803,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":17871,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f9631729-cea6-4fa4-8b9d-228d2f71bd70","uuid":"aac755e0-bde1-4fae-80fd-f340f4767000","request_id":"req_011CcPm9g58keEZzQRriAmXS"}
